  ok   a YES ask at 95c inside the band is the bet
  ok   so is a NO ask at 93c -- the side is whichever one is expensive
  ok   NULL: 99c is above the default window; the win no longer pays for the fee
  ok   ...but GOLD's own window runs to 99c: the grid found 98-99c loses 0.0-0.1% inside 15 s
  ok   NULL: 99.5c is above even GOLD's window
  ok   WTI's window runs to 60 s: the grid found 95-99c loses 0.0-1.5% at every horizon to 60
  ok   NULL: 55 s is outside GOLD's window, where 95-98c loses 10%
  ok   an explicit price window overrides the band's
  ok   NULL: 80c is below the window -- not a near-certainty
  ok   NULL: 20 s is outside GOLD's band, where the tape loses 7.69%
  ok   NULL: 1 s left is inside nothing -- an order could land after the close
  ok   ...but 20 s IS inside WTI's band, which the tape says is its best
  ok   NULL: a stale book is refused, not traded on
  ok   NULL: a book flagged suspect after a reconnect is refused
  ok   NULL: no book at all is not a signal
  ok   NULL: a price with nothing behind it is not an offer
  ok   with both sides quoted the expensive one is the near-certainty
  ok   the fee is Kalshi's quadratic, not a guess
  ok   settlement YES: the YES bet won and the NO bet lost
  ok   a win pays (1 - price) minus the fee, times the contracts
  ok   a loss costs the whole price, plus the fee
  ok   NULL: no settlement on file scores nothing, rather than assuming a win
  ok   the summary splits by series, which is the whole comparison
  ok   NULL: nothing scored -> nothing reported
  ok   GOLD near: 2-15 s at 95-99c only (the 90-95c cells there lose 5-9%), skipping the COMEX session 08-14 ET (32 mkts/2 lost inside, 117/0 outside)
  ok   GOLD far: 91-180 s at 95-99c -- 379 markets, 2 lost
  ok   the close's ET hour is read from the ticker's own clock
  ok   NULL at 11:00 ET; a bet at 03:00 and at 14:00 -- the skip is [8, 14)
  ok   NULL: 92c is below gold's near window, which the table says loses 9%
  ok   the far window buys at 120 s in ANY hour and refuses 60 s -- the dead middle
  ok   WTI: 95-99c to 60 s; 90-95c only 16-45 s; and 121-180 s at 90-99c, whose 90-95c corner is the best commodity cell found (105 mkts, 3 lost)
  ok   a window override parses to a band and names itself after the cell
  ok   ...and the 2-30 arm takes at 5 s, which wti-sweet refuses -- that is the whole comparison the operator asked for
  ok   prices in CENTS, a backwards clock, a backwards band and a short spec are all REFUSED rather than guessed at -- 93 read as a dollar floor would trade nothing and look like a dead cell instead of a typo (hard rule 5)
  ok   and 16-30 s at 93-99c, which oilband found beats the LIVE window at every price it shares with it
  ok   the sweet window starts at 16 s: under 95c INSIDE 15 s is the one oil cell that fails its own break-even (3 of 43 closes, 6.98% vs 5.61%)
  ok   98c IS inside it -- the band was widened up rather than the clock widened down, which buys back the volume without buying the losses (86 closes against the live rule's 72, still one loss)
  ok   NULL: 92c is below the floor -- under 93c oil loses 4.8% to 10.8% of closes even in this window
  ok   WTI far starts at 121 s: 91-120 s is negative for oil except at 98-99c
  ok   SILVER has a real strategy after all -- far out it is as good as gold (187 markets, 2 lost). Its near window was the mistake, not the series
  ok   and the NEGATIVE CONTROL is now a cell the grid says LOSES 5-12%, which is a real control; if paper wins there, nothing here is trusted
  ok   COPPER's only positive zone is the MIDDLE, where every other series is worst: 227 markets, 2 lost
  ok   NATGAS gets its single best cell (+0.14c) rather than being dropped
  ok   the look records span every window of the series
  ok   no window runs above 99c; the DEFAULT stays at the live bot's 98c
  ok   all five series are traded in paper now, and every window carries its label
  ok   the trade loop passes the close's ET hour into decide and limits bets per market per WINDOW
  ok   PAPER ONLY: 'pintake' appears nowhere in the working code
  ok   PAPER ONLY: 'ordercli' appears nowhere in the working code
  ok   PAPER ONLY: 'post_only' appears nowhere in the working code
  ok   PAPER ONLY: '/portfolio/orders' appears nowhere in the working code
  ok   the REPO's livebook is loaded, not the scratch copy in kals-work that shadows it and runs an analysis on import (currently C:\kals-repo\research\livebook.py)
  ok   and the repo path is searched BEFORE kals-work is added, which is what keeps it that way
